Linux project Code 
file management system 
#!/bin/bash

# Create a directory
mkdir -p FileProject

# Enter the directory
cd FileProject || exit

# Create first file
touch file1.txt

# Write data into file
echo "Hello Linux" > file1.txt

# Display file contents
cat file1.txt

# Create second file
touch file2.txt

# Copy file1.txt into file3.txt
cp file1.txt file3.txt

# Rename file2.txt
mv file2.txt newfile.txt

# Display final files
echo "Final Files:"
ls -l
4. Commands Used
mkdir: Creates a new directory.
mkdir -p: Creates a directory along with its parent directories if required.
cd: Changes the current working directory.
touch: Creates an empty file.
echo: Displays text or writes text into a file.
cat: Displays the contents of a file.
cp: Copies one file into another file.
mv: Moves or renames a file.
rm: Removes a file.
ls: Lists files and directories.
ls -l: Displays files with detailed information.
5. Procedure
First, the mkdir command is used to create a directory named fileproject. The cd command is used to enter the directory. The touch command creates file1.txt, and the echo command writes the message "hello linux" into it. The cat command displays the saved text.
Next, another file named file2.txt is created using touch. The ls command displays the files present in the directory. The cp command copies file1.txt into file3.txt. The mv command renames file2.txt to newfile.txt. The rm command removes the unwanted file. Finally, ls -l displays the remaining files with their details.
